        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-ababfd1f03dfe1fa\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":3,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Cyrano’s “Je t’aime” is unequivocally a sincere avowal of his loving state (T). It also has independently supported expressive force: he explicitly abandons polished artifice and describes himself as flinging out whatever words come to him, with the utterance emerging amid emotional overwhelm (E). It does not itself undertake a relational commitment, and T plus E adequately represent its core force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The degree of E is somewhat interpretive because this remains an eloquent dramatic declaration; however, the explicit framing as an unarranged outpouring from the heart supports more than merely passionate delivery. Roxane’s mistaken attribution of the speech to Christian complicates its reception, not its core force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Cyrano directly says “Je t’aime” to Roxane from beneath the balcony, but concealed by darkness and effectively speaking in Christian’s place, so Roxane misidentifies the lover whose feelings are being avowed.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration is sincere as Cyrano’s avowal but occurs within an identity deception: Roxane understands the voice and words as Christian’s. Cyrano stresses that he is finally speaking from his “true heart,” without the wit and artifice that previously screened his feelings. His promise to throw out the words as they come, “without arranging them in a bouquet,” and the surrounding language of suffocation and emotional excess present the words partly as affective overflow. The subsequent elaboration confirms enduring, attentive, self-sacrificing love, while Roxane explicitly recognizes the speech as love. None of this makes the highlighted words a relational undertaking by Cyrano, since he remains hidden and cannot openly establish such a commitment with her.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je t’aime, je suis fou, je n’en peux plus, c’est trop.”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration and its immediate elaboration strongly support a truth-conditional avowal of Cyrano’s loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Mon langage jamais jusqu’ici n’est sorti / De mon vrai cœur” and “j’ose être enfin moi-même.”\",\"supports\":\"Cyrano explicitly frames the speech as sincere self-revelation issuing from his own heart, supporting T despite Roxane’s mistaken identification of him.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Tous ceux… / Qui me viendront, je vais vous les jeter, en touffe, / Sans les mettre en bouquet.”\",\"supports\":\"He presents the words as arriving and being cast out without deliberate arrangement, giving positive evidence for expressive/reflexive force beyond emotional intensity alone.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Stage directions describe Cyrano as increasingly overcome, feverish, distracted, and deeply shaken; he also calls the feared sincere expression “un élan.”\",\"supports\":\"Together with his metalinguistic description of uncensored speech, these details reinforce E as affective outpouring rather than merely emphatic assertion.\",\"confidence\":0.88},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane responds, “Oui, c’est bien de l’amour,” and Cyrano answers that the feeling invading him “c’est vraiment / De l’amour.”\",\"supports\":\"The interlocutors explicitly identify the disclosed state as love, strongly corroborating T.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev6\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cyrano speaks protected by darkness while Christian remains beneath the balcony and later interjects; Roxane responds as though the declaration comes from her expected suitor.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes the identity-deception complication while leaving the utterance a direct and sincere expression of Cyrano’s own feelings.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev7\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Edmond Rostand’s Cyrano de Bergerac.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the linguistic form and bibliographic setting of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the sincere avowal and E captures the textually foregrounded release of previously screened feeling. The concealed identity and mistaken attribution are contextual complications rather than missing dimensions of core force; no residual O pressure remains.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
